1
00:00:11,383 --> 00:00:37,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a few more sutta excerpts that we have here, and these, well, they cover various topics, which we also addressed in some of our previous talks, but it won't hurt to go through it again, maybe say some other things about it, help people think about it from a different angle.

2
00:00:38,971 --> 00:01:02,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first excerpt is from Anguttara Nikaya 6, 68, and it goes, Because that a bhikkhu takes pleasure in company, who delights in company, who is engaged in the pleasure of company, who takes pleasure in groups, who delights in groups, who is engaged in the pleasure of groups, that he will find pleasure in seclusion when alone, that is not possible.

3
00:01:04,854 --> 00:01:12,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Not finding pleasure in seclusion when alone, that he will pick up the sign of the mind, cittanimitta, this is not possible.

4
00:01:13,748 --> 00:01:19,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Not picking up the sign of the mind, that he would fulfill the right view, that's not possible.

5
00:01:20,217 --> 00:01:26,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having fulfilled the right view, that he would fulfill the right composure, the samādhi, that's not possible.

6
00:01:27,282 --> 00:01:31,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having fulfilled the right composure, that he will abandon the fetters, that is not possible.

7
00:01:32,086 --> 00:01:36,569
[SPEAKER_00]: And not having abandoned the fetters, that he will realize nibbāna, that is not possible.

8
00:01:44,656 --> 00:01:45,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

9
00:01:48,665 --> 00:01:59,613
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole point here, as it can be seen, is that one needs to have grasped, picked up sign of the mind.

10
00:02:00,493 --> 00:02:13,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that might sound sort of mystical to somebody, but sign of the mind, cittanimitta, literally means the characteristics, signs, tokens of that which mind is.

11
00:02:14,453 --> 00:02:20,215
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not having the sign of the mind, that's that's the state of an average person's mind.

12
00:02:20,295 --> 00:02:30,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to delight, you need to first make effort to avoid company, dwell in solitude and then find pleasure in that seclusion.

13
00:02:30,377 --> 00:02:35,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, literally self-deferring seclusion over company, regardless of the company.

14
00:02:37,113 --> 00:02:41,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's the best possible company, you would still prefer seclusion.

15
00:02:41,616 --> 00:02:53,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is a necessary basis to pick, to start picking and discerning these characteristics, tokens, properties of that which mind is.

16
00:02:55,189 --> 00:03:09,210
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, and the important difference here, it's not like that you would sit down and psychologize and sort of contrive these various properties that you think belong to the mind, but

17
00:03:10,191 --> 00:03:14,495
[SPEAKER_00]: genuinely discern them for what they are on the level of the mind.

18
00:03:14,615 --> 00:03:17,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not putting them there, you're seeing them for what they are.

19
00:03:17,478 --> 00:03:30,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're looking for the properties of the mind, which means you're developing, basically, a certain recognition of that which mind is, because mind is defined by its properties, the sign of the mind.

20
00:03:33,236 --> 00:03:43,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So you want to... how to discern the characteristics of the mind, you need to have been dwelling in solitude and not in company for a sufficient amount of time.

21
00:03:45,752 --> 00:03:52,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably work through boredom, anxiety, restlessness and all the other hindrances.

22
00:03:54,332 --> 00:04:23,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you have worked through it, you start developing, you start preferring that solitude, you start developing the pleasure in it, in that seclusion, and on account of that, or around, let's say, around that time, you could then start... well, by that time you would be more familiar, let's put it like that, with that which mind is, because you wouldn't be distracting yourself from it,

23
00:04:23,908 --> 00:04:31,629
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be engaging with centrality or in company, so you leave yourself no other choice but to be living with your own mind.

24
00:04:31,749 --> 00:04:43,512
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start living with your own mind, which is what most people avoid on the day-to-day basis, if you start living with your own mind, you will then start discerning its inherent characteristics.

25
00:04:44,752 --> 00:04:49,873
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the necessary basis to become a Sotāpanna, to develop the right view.

26
00:04:50,053 --> 00:04:51,133
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no right view

27
00:04:52,110 --> 00:05:03,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Without that sign of the mind, without knowing what the mind is, without seeing it as a phenomenon, it's impossible to develop the right view, unless you know exactly what your mind is.

28
00:05:04,242 --> 00:05:06,704
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, understand it, see that sign of the mind.

29
00:05:06,904 --> 00:05:10,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is what mind as a phenomenon, as a thing is.

30
00:05:14,455 --> 00:05:17,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And if a person is puzzled, like, what is there to understand?

31
00:05:17,596 --> 00:05:26,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, most people's views about the mind are the views that they're derived from the senses, that then you apply to your mind.

32
00:05:27,119 --> 00:05:35,161
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why often people approach mind, like, scientifically, medically, even psychologically.

33
00:05:35,961 --> 00:05:37,542
[SPEAKER_00]: But none of those things are the mind.

34
00:05:37,602 --> 00:05:39,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind precedes those things.

35
00:05:39,142 --> 00:05:44,044
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot accurately describe, discern, see them present,

36
00:05:45,548 --> 00:05:55,439
[SPEAKER_00]: As they are characteristics of the mind, eh... by using the sense... sense data and inputs... and input of your senses, because the mind does not appear in any of the senses.

37
00:05:57,001 --> 00:05:58,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then the mind?

38
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:01,761
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing mystical.

39
00:06:02,021 --> 00:06:13,650
[SPEAKER_00]: If something's mystical about it, it's because the whole direction is too opaque, because of the people inevitably sort of spending their life distracting themselves from their own mind.

40
00:06:13,710 --> 00:06:22,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the slightest thought in the direction of what the mind might be, on its own terms, it's just overwhelmingly ambiguous.

41
00:06:22,897 --> 00:06:26,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's so hard to see the mind and understand the mind, as the Buddha said in the other suttas.

42
00:06:29,006 --> 00:06:32,488
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to do so, you need to do so if you want to get the right view.

43
00:06:33,689 --> 00:06:40,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not picking up the sign of the mind that Bhikkhu would fulfill right view, that's simply not possible.

44
00:06:41,193 --> 00:06:42,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

45
00:06:44,495 --> 00:06:52,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And that should also shed some light on people who kind of have their... can't help but hope for some special experiences,

46
00:06:52,999 --> 00:07:12,591
[SPEAKER_00]: To come out of the meditation or practice and so on, including like the attainments of the Sotāpārthi and higher, more often than not, it's assumed to be this overwhelmingly kind of entering this experience, and you can't put it into words and so on.

47
00:07:13,531 --> 00:07:19,975
[SPEAKER_00]: But, whatever that experience might be, you want to ask yourself, well, have I understood

48
00:07:21,021 --> 00:07:25,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is a phenomena, on account of which I had this experience.

49
00:07:27,545 --> 00:07:31,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you can't put it in the words, means you haven't understood it, as simple as that.

50
00:07:31,947 --> 00:07:37,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever experience it was, doesn't matter how pleasant, how uplifting, how out of this world it was, it's irrelevant.

51
00:07:38,329 --> 00:07:42,110
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the experience you should be, eh... seeking out.

52
00:07:42,410 --> 00:07:43,370
[SPEAKER_00]: If it happens, happens.

53
00:07:43,731 --> 00:07:44,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Not your problem.

54
00:07:44,991 --> 00:07:48,332
[SPEAKER_00]: What you want to seek out is understanding the sign of the mind.

55
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:52,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you can develop and understand the right view.

56
00:07:53,054 --> 00:07:54,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not given.

57
00:07:54,134 --> 00:07:56,155
[SPEAKER_00]: That does not occur spontaneously.

58
00:07:57,192 --> 00:08:00,393
[SPEAKER_00]: That occurs through your own effort of that development.

59
00:08:00,573 --> 00:08:08,895
[SPEAKER_00]: So, fulfilling then the right view, or rather, not having fulfilled the right view, that he would have samādhi, that's not possible.

60
00:08:08,975 --> 00:08:09,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another thing.

61
00:08:10,715 --> 00:08:19,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Very common misconception is that you can do samādhi correctly, you can practice Buddhist meditation correctly, knowing that you don't even have the right view.

62
00:08:21,123 --> 00:08:28,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the usual argument for that is, well, my meditation helps me.

63
00:08:28,668 --> 00:08:31,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody says it's not helpful, but it doesn't mean it's right.

64
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:39,875
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean it's on the level of freeing you from suffering that the Buddha said is the beginning of the dhamma, sotāpaṭṭhi.

65
00:08:40,555 --> 00:08:42,136
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the level you should be aiming for.

66
00:08:42,196 --> 00:08:47,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that you can practice samādhi without sotāpaṭṭhi, it's not possible.

67
00:08:47,499 --> 00:08:48,700
[SPEAKER_00]: That you can practice correctly.

68
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:56,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't have the right view, doesn't matter how helpful your samādhi is, it's not the right samādhi.

69
00:08:56,923 --> 00:09:14,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Because right samādhi is not determined by what you deem helpfulness is, it's determined by the right view, which is determined by grasping the sign of the mind, discerning the mind as a thing, as a phenomenon, on its own terms, which is redetermined by finding pleasure in seclusion.

70
00:09:15,647 --> 00:09:32,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just forcing yourself in seclusion, which is determined by not being engaged with groups, not delighting in groups, not delighting in people, not delighting in camaraderie, in company, in hanging out and so on.

71
00:09:32,053 --> 00:09:35,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having developed the right view, you can then develop the right samādhi.

72
00:09:36,585 --> 00:09:38,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's basically what right samādhi is.

73
00:09:39,547 --> 00:09:44,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Composing that right view to the extent necessary for final knowledge, for arahantryu.

74
00:09:44,650 --> 00:09:52,335
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it says, furthermore, not having fulfilled that right samādhi, that that monk would abandon the fetters, i.e.

75
00:09:52,435 --> 00:09:54,336
[SPEAKER_00]: become an arahant, that's not possible.

76
00:09:56,458 --> 00:09:59,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having abandoned the fetters that he would realize nibbāna, that's not possible.

77
00:10:01,685 --> 00:10:03,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, consequently, it just goes the other way.

78
00:10:04,026 --> 00:10:14,889
[SPEAKER_00]: A bhikkhu who doesn't delight, doesn't take pleasure in company, doesn't delight in company, isn't engaged in company, it is possible for him to find pleasure in seclusion when alone.

79
00:10:14,949 --> 00:10:19,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding pleasure in seclusion when alone, that he will pick up the sign of the mind, that is possible.

80
00:10:19,670 --> 00:10:20,611
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not guaranteed.

81
00:10:20,831 --> 00:10:24,352
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs to make the effort, but it is possible, because there is a basis.

82
00:10:24,412 --> 00:10:28,073
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a fertile basis for him picking up the sign of the mind.

83
00:10:30,838 --> 00:10:34,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Picking up sign of the mind, that he will fulfill the right view, that is also possible.

84
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:37,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Having fulfilled the right view, that he will fulfill right samādhi, that is also possible.

85
00:10:40,324 --> 00:10:43,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Having fulfilled the right samādhi, that he would abandon the fetters, that is possible.

86
00:10:43,646 --> 00:10:46,368
[SPEAKER_00]: And having abandoned the fetters, that he will realize nibbāna, that is possible.

87
00:10:56,354 --> 00:10:56,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why any

88
00:11:00,407 --> 00:11:02,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And this applies to lay people and monks.

89
00:11:02,570 --> 00:11:05,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, obviously to monks, but applies also to lay people.

90
00:11:06,695 --> 00:11:18,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And eh... any time that you have to practice the dhamma, to reflect, to read the suttas, to study, you should try and do it alone.

91
00:11:21,678 --> 00:11:23,560
[SPEAKER_00]: You should start cultivating seclusion.

92
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:39,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Any chance to take a break from your life, in a sense of a retreat or going away from the usual busy environment, you should strive for the environment that would be solitary.

93
00:11:41,494 --> 00:11:42,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it takes effort.

94
00:11:43,035 --> 00:11:50,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, these things that are described in this sutta, they're not... they're not automatic, as I said before.

95
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:56,225
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you just need to extract yourself from the company and you will then automatically start finding pleasure and seclusion.

96
00:11:56,265 --> 00:11:56,665
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

97
00:11:57,986 --> 00:11:59,387
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be in the company.

98
00:12:00,367 --> 00:12:07,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be sort of separated from the group, but you would still be valuing that as a pleasure.

99
00:12:08,899 --> 00:12:11,402
[SPEAKER_00]: You would still be valuing company as pleasant.

100
00:12:12,283 --> 00:12:16,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You would still be carrying mental delight in company, even if you're not engaging in it physically.

101
00:12:18,829 --> 00:12:23,614
[SPEAKER_00]: So you want to make the effort to uproot the pleasure that you find in company.

102
00:12:23,654 --> 00:12:25,056
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that, you need to be alone.

103
00:12:26,697 --> 00:12:32,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Having uprooted the pleasure that you find in company, then you can start finding pleasure in seclusion.

104
00:12:33,428 --> 00:12:34,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it's not automatic.

105
00:12:35,249 --> 00:12:37,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion... so it needs work.

106
00:12:37,791 --> 00:12:45,577
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to put in the work in order to start regarding seclusion as pleasant, feeling it agreeably.

107
00:12:46,517 --> 00:12:48,899
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't put in the work, it will not be agreeable.

108
00:12:51,621 --> 00:12:53,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know whether it's agreeable or not.

109
00:12:53,402 --> 00:12:57,765
[SPEAKER_00]: If... when you are secluded, just see what your mind does, how you're reacting.

110
00:12:57,785 --> 00:12:58,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you looking for distraction?

111
00:12:59,046 --> 00:13:00,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you bearing?

112
00:13:00,427 --> 00:13:01,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you feeling like you're drowning?

113
00:13:03,938 --> 00:13:07,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you really, really at ease simply by being alone?

114
00:13:07,359 --> 00:13:09,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what you should be striving for.

115
00:13:09,499 --> 00:13:21,881
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, even before you get to the point of picking up the sign of the mind, as this sutta describes, you already have to put in a lot of work, a lot of enduring, a lot of withdrawal, a lot of reflection.

116
00:13:24,962 --> 00:13:27,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just in order to find pleasure in seclusion.

117
00:13:28,642 --> 00:13:30,383
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can pick up the sign of the mind.

118
00:13:30,943 --> 00:13:32,843
[SPEAKER_00]: It will basically outline itself.

119
00:13:34,155 --> 00:13:37,837
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you get to discern it, the characteristics of the mind.

120
00:13:37,857 --> 00:13:47,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you get to know the mind at the time, in whatever state or mood it is, for what it is.

121
00:13:47,662 --> 00:13:53,145
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another, then, layer of work and effort you have to apply.

122
00:13:55,927 --> 00:14:10,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it goes to the right view, which is described in the suttas, obviously, in different ways, but it's usually the Four Noble Truths, understanding the way of suffering, understanding the nature of suffering, the escape from it, and so on.

123
00:14:11,941 --> 00:14:19,045
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, none of these things occur to you spontaneously.

124
00:14:19,805 --> 00:14:26,549
[SPEAKER_00]: None of these things can occur just against or out of your will, or outside of your will.

125
00:14:29,511 --> 00:14:32,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, none of those things can occur out of your meditation.

126
00:14:33,613 --> 00:14:35,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as the next line says,

127
00:14:39,875 --> 00:14:43,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditating rightly, without a right view, is simply not possible.

128
00:14:44,198 --> 00:14:53,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your meditation, your seclusion, your striving, your effort, your study, should all be pointing to one thing.

129
00:14:53,304 --> 00:14:55,485
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other alternative, by the way.

130
00:14:56,466 --> 00:14:59,628
[SPEAKER_00]: They should all be pointing to getting the right view.

131
00:15:01,990 --> 00:15:04,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you'll know what to do.

132
00:15:04,391 --> 00:15:06,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not like, oh, there's just one approach.

133
00:15:07,315 --> 00:15:07,956
[SPEAKER_00]: To the dhamma.

134
00:15:08,216 --> 00:15:10,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, here we teach the right view.

135
00:15:10,558 --> 00:15:12,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Somewhere else... No, there is nothing else you can teach.

136
00:15:14,521 --> 00:15:16,082
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no dhamma without the right view.

137
00:15:16,102 --> 00:15:18,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the right view is not the goal, dhamma is not the goal.

138
00:15:19,024 --> 00:15:19,785
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

139
00:15:22,927 --> 00:15:31,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And for those who wonder how fundamental the right view is, you can read Majjhima Nikāya's Samādhīti Sutta, the Right View Sutta.

140
00:15:33,222 --> 00:15:35,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It determines the whole path, the right view.

141
00:15:36,764 --> 00:15:37,805
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not really optional.

142
00:15:38,025 --> 00:15:41,787
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not really negotiable.

143
00:15:41,987 --> 00:15:55,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why we keep insisting that it's important to kind of admit and keep admitting this to yourself, that without the right view there is no dhamma, it's because it's so easy to develop a false sense of security.

144
00:15:55,875 --> 00:15:57,076
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, I mean, there are

145
00:15:58,652 --> 00:16:15,330
[SPEAKER_00]: There are teachings and meditation techniques and so on that can make you peaceful and help you, but... it's risky in a sense that you can then easily jump into the view that it's because it's helpful, it's the right thing to do.

146
00:16:16,411 --> 00:16:19,515
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way to know the right thing to do is by having the right view.

147
00:16:21,318 --> 00:16:21,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

148
00:16:21,979 --> 00:16:28,624
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what experiences you might be having on account of your seclusion, pleasant, uplifting, out of this world.

149
00:16:29,265 --> 00:16:35,310
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to know that it's about knowing the right view, not about experiencing random things.

150
00:16:36,985 --> 00:16:54,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't keep that context in your mind, if you don't keep reminding yourself of it, you will then develop a view that you know what's right, what's helpful, what should be practiced, while at the same time you don't know, you don't have the right view.

151
00:16:55,955 --> 00:16:57,576
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the fundamental contradiction.

152
00:16:58,717 --> 00:17:00,738
[SPEAKER_00]: But because now you believe that you know,

153
00:17:01,754 --> 00:17:05,876
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be then making the effort to really find out what the right view is.

154
00:17:06,597 --> 00:17:18,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you allowed yourself a concession that you can know what dhamma is, you can practice what dhamma is, you can practice meditation according to the dhamma, without having the right view.

155
00:17:19,384 --> 00:17:27,828
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, just that belief, is basically a direct barricade to your progress towards the right view.

156
00:17:29,029 --> 00:17:30,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you do everything else rightly,

157
00:17:30,995 --> 00:17:34,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Having that belief will be the obstacle that will prevent you from the right view.

158
00:17:36,563 --> 00:17:41,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a contradiction in terms, and if you don't abandon it, you will not be able to see it for yourself.

